Why a DNS Domain helps (The "Usability" Factor)

You asked how a DNS domain would help. For your non-technical users, this is the difference between a "professional" experience and a "technical" one.

Without a local DNS domain:
Your users have to remember IP addresses and port numbers. To check their photos, they have to type:
http://10.0.2.15:2283
If the IP changes or the port is confusing, they will call you for help every time.

With a local DNS domain (e.g., woogle.net or home.arpa):
You use AdGuard Home (which is in your plan) to create a "DNS Rewrite." You tell AdGuard: "Whenever someone types photos.home, send them to 10.0.2.15." Then, Nginx Proxy Manager (NPM) handles the port.
The user simply types:
http://photos.home

The Benefits:

    Zero Memory Load: Users only need to remember a word, not a string of numbers.
    Consistency: If you move the "Photos" service from one server to another, you only change the IP in one place (AdGuard), and the user's link (photos.home) never changes.
    Cleanliness: It removes the need for :8080 or :2283 at the end of every URL.

Recommendation: Use .home.arpa (the official standard for home networks) or a custom one like .woogle. Since this is internal and not on the public internet, you don't need to buy anything; you just "declare" it in AdGuard.
Level 1: The "Consultant" (Zero Access) — Recommended

In this model, the AI has no technical access to your servers, router, or NAS. It operates purely as a high-level architect.

    How it works: You describe the current state →→ The AI provides the exact command or YAML file →→ You copy and paste it into the terminal.
    Privilege Level: Zero.
    Security: Maximum. The AI cannot "go rogue" or make a mistake that affects your hardware because there is a "Human-in-the-Loop" (you) acting as the firewall.
    What the AI needs: It only needs the text files from your Git repos and the output of commands you run (e.g., you run ip a, copy the result, and paste it into the chat).

Level 2: The "Operator" (Restricted Agent Access)

If you use n8n or a similar agent framework and want the AI to actually deploy the changes, you must create a "sandbox" for it. Do not give the AI your root password or a full sudoer account.

If you go this route, apply these three restrictions:

    Dedicated Service Account: Create a specific user for the AI (e.g., ai-deploy). Do not use your primary admin account.
    Restricted Sudo: Instead of giving the AI full sudo access, edit the /etc/sudoers file to allow the ai-deploy user to only run specific commands (e.g., docker compose up, systemctl restart nginx).
    The "Confirmation Gate": If using n8n, do not let the AI trigger a "Shell Execute" node automatically. Set up a "Wait for Approval" node where the AI presents the command it wants to run, and you must click "Approve" before it executes.

