Final Verdict for WoogleNet

Because you are building a network specifically designed for security and simplicity, I strongly recommend Level 1 (The Consultant).

The "cost" is that you have to copy and paste commands, but the "gain" is the absolute certainty that no AI error can accidentally wipe your NAS or open a hole in your UDM-Pro firewall. The AI provides the "recipe," and you remain the "chef."


Some potnetial issues with the design that need to be addressed 
1. The "Single Point of Failure" (The JAX Problem)

In your current plan, JAX is the brain. It handles DNS (AdGuard) and the Proxy (NPM).

    The Risk: If the JAX host crashes or has a disk failure, your non-technical users won't just lose one app—they will lose everything, including the ability to resolve google.com because DNS is down.
    The Low-Cost Fix:
        AdGuard Home Secondary: Deploy a second, tiny AdGuard instance on SAX. In the UDM-Pro DHCP settings, you can list two DNS servers. If JAX goes down, the network stays online.
        Configuration Sync: Use a simple rsync or a Git-based backup of your AdGuard and NPM configs so you can restore them to another host in minutes.

2. The "Silent Failure" (Monitoring)

Right now, you will only know a service is down when a user tells you, "The photos app isn't working." For a "pro" feel, you need to know before they do.

    The Recommendation: Deploy Uptime Kuma.
        What it is: A free, self-hosted monitoring tool with a beautiful dashboard.
        Why it fits: It pings your services (and your NAS) every 60 seconds. If something goes down, it can send you a notification (via Discord, Telegram, or Gotify—all free).
        Usability: You can add an "Uptime" link to your Homepage dashboard so users can see if "The System" is having issues before they complain.

3. The "Maintenance Debt" (Auto-Updates)

Updating 10+ Docker containers across multiple hosts (JAX, SAX, Proxmox) becomes a chore very quickly. You will either forget to update (creating security holes) or spend every Saturday doing it manually.

    The Recommendation: Deploy Watchtower.
        What it is: A lightweight container that monitors your other containers. When a new version of an image (like Nextcloud) is released, Watchtower automatically pulls the new image and restarts the container.
        The Security Catch: Only use Watchtower for "stable" apps. For critical apps (like Vaultwarden), configure Watchtower to only notify you that an update is available, so you can update those manually after a backup.

4. The "Last Mile" of Usability (mDNS/Bonjour)

You've solved the DNS problem with AdGuard, but there is a "hidden" frustration in home networks: Printers and Casting.

    The Gotcha: Devices like iPhones (AirPrint) or Chromecasts use mDNS (Multicast DNS), which typically does not cross VLAN boundaries. If your user is on the "Trusted LAN" and the printer is on the "IoT VLAN," they might not "see" it, even if the firewall allows the traffic.
    The Fix: Ensure the mDNS Reflector/Repeater is enabled on your UDM-Pro. This allows the "discovery" packets to jump between your VLANs so the non-technical user's phone can find the printer without them needing to type in an IP address.

