UPS Monitoring
Since I  have 5 different UPS units across multiple brands, I will need a centralized NUT server to coordinate the shutdown. If I don't, I will have to manage 5 different shutdown scripts, which is a recipe for a corrupted database during a power outage.
Do not install the NUT server directly on your Proxmox hosts. Instead, create a privileged Debian or Ubuntu LXC container on your most stable R720 node.

    Why an LXC? It is lightweight, easy to back up, and allows you to pass through USB devices from the host to the container.
    The Setup:
        Connect the UPSs to the physical host via USB.
        Use Proxmox USB Passthrough to map those devices into the NUT LXC.
        Install nut-server in the LXC. This becomes your NUT Master.
        Install nut-client on every Proxmox host (DAX, MAX, PAX) and your bare-metal SAX server. These are your NUT Slaves.

Handling Multiple Brands (Drivers)

NUT is excellent because it abstracts the brand. In your ups.conf file on the Master, you will define each UPS separately:

    For the APCs: Use the usbhid-ups driver.
    For the Eaton/Tripp Lite: Use the usbhid-ups or blazer_usb driver.

Each UPS will have its own name (e.g., ups-core, ups-gpu, ups-network). You can then configure the Master to trigger a global shutdown if any of the critical UPS units hit a "Low Battery" state.
The Architectural Shutdown Chain

To prevent data corruption (especially for your NAS and Proxmox VMs), you must configure the shutdown order. If the hosts shut down before the VMs, you risk filesystem corruption.

The recommended logic flow:

    UPS Hits Critical: NUT Master detects low battery.
    Signal Sent: Master sends the shutdown command to all Slaves.
    Guest Shutdown: Proxmox hosts receive the signal and trigger a graceful shutdown of all running VMs/Containers (using the Proxmox "Shutdown" timeout).
    Host Shutdown: Once VMs are off, the Proxmox hosts shut themselves down.
    Final Kill: The NUT Master (LXC) shuts down last, and the physical host it resides on follows.

Summary Checklist for Implementation

    Hardware: Ensure all 5 UPSs are connected via USB to a host that can pass them to the LXC.
    LXC: Create a privileged LXC →→ Install nut-server.
    Config: Map each UPS in ups.conf using the usbhid-ups driver.
    Clients: Install nut-client on DAX, MAX, PAX, and SAX.
    Test: Perform a "dry run" (simulated power failure) to ensure the SAX server and Proxmox nodes actually trigger a shutdown.

